  "body": "## Objective\nTurn the WebGPU renderer into a bounded quality ladder with Medium as the primary GTX 1650 Super 1080p/60 design target.\n\n## Scope\nLow/Medium/High/Ultra preset policy for self-shadow, DSO, Dark Bloom, contact and later reserved effects; benchmark all canonical scenes on target hardware; tune bounded secondary quality without compromising core albedo/depth/object ownership; document mean/p95 GPU results.\n\n## Non-goals\nNo adaptive quality controller (SM-802), no hidden target weakening.\n\n## Dependencies / concurrency\nDepends on SM-500 and feature-complete renderer intended for the release; consumes SM-003 WebGL2 baseline.\n\n## Canonical context\n`docs/WEBGPU_VALIDATION_PLAN.md` §§16–18; `docs/LIGHTING_FIDELITY_ROADMAP.md` quality presets.\n\n## Implementation prompt\nTreat ≤12 ms mean and ≤14.5 ms p95 at 1080p as targets. Tune secondary-effect samples/resolutions/culling first. Preserve native core ownership/material data. If evidence shows the target is infeasible for a justified reason, open/update a decision record rather than silently changing it.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- Presets have explicit bounded settings/caps.\n- Medium remains visually representative, not a broken fallback.\n- Target-hardware benchmark report contains three-run distributions and workload metadata.\n- WebGPU result is compared with SM-003 WebGL2 baseline.\n\n## Verification\nCanonical benchmark methodology (300 warm-up, ≥600 measured, three runs where practical) in Chrome and at least a correctness/performance spot-check in Firefox.\n\n## Expected artifacts\nQuality preset implementation, GTX1650S benchmark dataset/report, graphics UI/diagnostics updates.\n\n## Blocking / stopping conditions\nIf target hardware is unavailable or GPU timestamps unavailable, do not close performance acceptance; record the blocker and continue only non-hardware preset work.",
